module m

// FIXED in main on 2026-08-18 as #725 -- this file is now a REGRESSION
// fixture: `nova check` must REFUSE it. If it ever passes again, the
// hole is back.
//
// What it showed while it was open: an arm carrying an OR-pattern
// disabled the exhaustiveness check. `Violet` has no arm here, and
// `nova check` said nothing (exit 0); built and run, the program
// answered `0` for the uncovered variant.
//
// The integrator's diagnosis, recorded because it explains the shape:
// the checker understood three arm forms and everything else fell into
// a conservative `_ => return` (doctrine #703, and that doctrine is
// right). `p | q | r` is parsed exactly, so counting it among the
// "not understood" was an unfinished branch, not caution. Arm parsing
// now lives in `arm_coverage` (CatchAll/Variants/Unknown); an unknown
// alternative inside `|` still voids the whole arm, or the refusal
// would be false.
//
// The measurement that found it (arms written one per name were already
// refused; the OR forms were not):
//
//     Red => 1 / Green => 2 / Blue => 3 / Amber => 4
//         -> E_MATCH_NON_EXHAUSTIVE, correct
//     Red => 1 / Green | Blue | Amber => 0
//         -> accepted, WRONG
//     the same, wrapped over two lines
//         -> accepted, WRONG
//     an OR-group with every variant covered
//         -> accepted, correct
//
// Why it matters beyond the miss itself: novac is built on the promise
// that a NEW variant of a sum breaks the build at every place that must
// decide about it. Wherever an OR-group is used to group the "nothing to
// do" cases -- which is the idiomatic way to write a table -- that
// promise silently does not hold.
//
// Found 2026-08-18 by a table of 64 token kinds that was missing two of
// them and compiled clean.
//
// And the program does not even fail loudly: built and run, this file
// prints `0` for `Colour.Violet` -- the uncovered variant yields the zero
// value of the result type and says nothing. A miss that crashes is a
// nuisance; a miss that answers with a plausible number is a defect that
// travels.

type Colour enum Red | Green | Blue | Amber | Violet

fn code(c Colour) -> int {
    match c {
        Red => 1
        Green | Blue | Amber => 0
    }
}

fn main() { println(code(Colour.Violet)) }
